    "full_story_part1": {
      "status": "OK",
      "text": "A longer life can sound like a very big project. More sleep. More exercise. Better food. But a 2026 study gives a quieter picture. It suggests that very small differences, when put together, may matter. Researchers led by the University of Sydney studied 59,078 people in the UK Biobank. The study was published in *eClinicalMedicine*. Sleep and physical activity were measured for 7 days with a device worn on the wrist. Diet quality received a score from zero to 100. The middle follow-up time was 8.1 years. The researchers then used a statistical model. It looked at how different mixes of sleep, activity, and diet were linked with lifespan and healthspan. One small mix stood out.",
