      "fallback_used": false,
      "canonical_text": "A longer life can sound like a very big project.\n\nMore sleep. More exercise. Better food.\n\nBut a 2026 study gives a quieter picture. It suggests that very small differences, when put together, may matter.\n\nResearchers led by the University of Sydney studied 59,078 people in the UK Biobank. The study was published in *eClinicalMedicine*.\n\nSleep and physical activity were measured for seven days with a device worn on the wrist. Diet quality received a score from zero to 100. The middle follow-up time was 8.1 years.\n\nThe researchers then used a statistical model. It looked at how different mixes of sleep, activity, and diet were linked with lifespan and healthspan.\n\nOne small mix stood out."
    },
    "full_story_part2": {
      "status": "OK",
      "text": "Compared with the lowest levels, one pattern included 5 more minutes of sleep each day. It also included about 2 more minutes of exercise at a medium or hard level. The diet quality score was 5 points higher. Together, these small differences were associated with about one more year of total life. There is an important detail here. This result was about lifespan—the full length of life. It did not mean one extra year of healthy life. For healthspan, a larger difference was needed before the model found a clear result. Healthspan means years lived without a major long-term disease. In that pattern, sleep was 24 minutes longer. Medium-to-hard activity was almost 4 minutes higher. The diet score was 23 points higher. This larger mix was associated with about 4 more years of healthy life.",
